        },
        "page_content": "The four truths are"
    },
    {
        "__document__": true,
        "metadata": {
            "jejune_source_name": "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md",
            "jejune_source_slug": "four-noble-truths",
            "paragraph_number": 2,
            "sentence_number": 1
        },
        "page_content": "Dukkha (not being at ease, 'suffering') is an innate characteristic of transient existence; nothing is forever, this is painful;"
    },
    {
        "__document__": true,
        "metadata": {
            "jejune_source_name": "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md",
            "jejune_source_slug": "four-noble-truths",
            "paragraph_number": 3,
            "sentence_number": 1
        },
        "page_content": "Samudaya (origin, cause): together with this transient world and its pain, there is also thirst (desire, longing, craving) for and attachment to this transient, unsatisfactory existence;"
    },
    {
        "__document__": true,
        "metadata": {
            "jejune_source_name": "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md",
            "jejune_source_slug": "four-noble-truths",
            "paragraph_number": 4,
            "sentence_number": 1
        },
        "page_content": "Nirodha (cessation, ending, confinement): the attachment to this transient world and its pain can be severed or contained by the confinement or letting go of this craving;"
    },
    {
        "__document__": true,
        "metadata": {
            "jejune_source_name": "250_BCE_-_Dhammacakkappavattana_Sutta_Four_Noble_Truths_Wikipedia_translation.md",
            "jejune_source_slug": "four-noble-truths",
            "paragraph_number": 5,
            "sentence_number": 1
        },
        "page_content": "Marga (road, path, way): the Noble Eightfold Path is the path leading to the confinement of this desire and attachment, and the release from dukkha."
    }
]